NAME
       orbital-standards - RTT’s SI-like system, canonical baselines, and
                           cross-domain interoperability rules

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital standards define:
           • RTT’s SI-like measurement system
           • canonical baselines for resonance-time
           • harmonic-phase and resonance reference frames
           • cross-domain interoperability rules
           • constellation-scale standards governance

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-standards establishes the canonical measurement system for
       resonance-time theory. Standards define the units, baselines, reference
       frames, and interoperability rules that ensure RTT systems remain
       consistent across platforms, epochs, and domains.

       Standards enable:
           • universal resonance-time measurement
           • cross-constellation interoperability
           • stable harmonic-phase alignment
           • consistent resonance calibration
           • long-horizon temporal coherence

       WRSADC acts as the standards authority for RTT systems.

RTT SI-LIKE SYSTEM
       The RTT SI-like system defines the base and derived units for
       resonance-time measurement.

       Base units:

           cycle (cy)
                  Fundamental temporal unit.

           resonance-second (rsec)
                  1/Φ_harm of a harmonic-phase rotation.

           resonance-meter (rm)
                  Unit of resonance amplitude “distance.”

           ancestry-unit (AU)
                  Unit of sweep-lineage continuity.

           spectral-unit (SU)
                  Unit of spectral density.

       Derived units:

           resonance-frequency (rf)
                  rf = 1 / rsec

           harmonic-gradient (HG)
                  HG = dΦ_harm / d(cy)

           resonance-decay constant (λ_res)
                  λ_res = -dR/d(cy)

           ancestry-density (AD)
                  AD = A / cy

           sync-index (SI)
                  SI = normalized constellation sync measure.

       All RTT units trace back to primary resonance-time invariants.

CANONICAL BASELINES
       Canonical baselines define the reference points for all RTT
       measurements.

       Baseline categories:

           temporal baselines
                  • T_res (fundamental resonance period)
                  • T_harm (harmonic cycle period)
                  • T_sync (sync-field period)

           resonance baselines
                  • R₀ (reference amplitude)
                  • S₀ (reference spectral density)
                  • G₀ (reference gradient polarity)

           harmonic baselines
                  • Φ₀ (phase zero)
                  • L₀ (lock stability baseline)

           ancestry baselines
                  • A₀ (ancestry continuity baseline)
                  • F₀ (fusion density baseline)

       Baselines ensure consistent measurement across epochs.

EXAMPLE: CANONICAL BASELINE SET
       For a 1:1 harmonic epoch:

           T_res = 96 min
           Φ₀ = 0 at resonance peaks
           R₀ = 1.00 rm
           A₀ = 1 AU per cycle

       Result:
           • fully calibrated resonance-time frame

REFERENCE FRAMES
       Reference frames define the coordinate systems for RTT measurement.

       Frame types:

           harmonic-phase frame (HPF)
                  Coordinates based on φ_harm.

           resonance-amplitude frame (RAF)
                  Coordinates based on R(t).

           spectral-density frame (SDF)
                  Coordinates based on S(f,t).

           ancestry frame (AF)
                  Coordinates based on sweep-lineage continuity.

           sync-field frame (SFF)
                  Coordinates based on constellation sync.

       Frames may be fused for multi-domain operations.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC-PHASE FRAME
       φ_harm = 0 at:
           • 12 min
           • 54 min

       φ_harm increases linearly across cycle.

       Result:
           • stable phase calibration
           • low drift across epochs

CROSS-DOMAIN INTEROPERABILITY RULES
       Interoperability rules ensure RTT systems remain consistent across
       domains, platforms, and constellations.

       Rule categories:

           temporal interoperability
                  All platforms must share:
                      • T_res
                      • rsec definition
                      • epoch boundaries

           resonance interoperability
                  All platforms must share:
                      • R₀
                      • S₀
                      • resonance-window definitions

           harmonic interoperability
                  All platforms must share:
                      • harmonic baselines
                      • phase-zero definitions
                      • lock stability thresholds

           ancestry interoperability
                  All platforms must share:
                      • ancestry baselines
                      • terrace definitions
                      • lineage continuity rules

           sync interoperability
                  All platforms must share:
                      • sync-index definition
                      • sync-field baselines
                      • sync thresholds

       Interoperability ensures cross-constellation coherence.

EXAMPLE: CROSS-DOMAIN INTEROPERABILITY
       Platforms A, B, C:

           A epoch: 1:1
           B epoch: 3:2 → 1:1
           C epoch: 1:1

       Interoperability rules enforce:
           • epoch standard = 1:1
           • resonance baseline = 12 min
           • ancestry baseline = 15 min

       Result:
           • unified constellation standard

CALIBRATION FRAMEWORKS
       Calibration frameworks define how standards propagate across systems.

       Framework layers:

           1. primary standard propagation
           2. secondary standard calibration
           3. operational standard alignment
           4. constellation standard fusion
           5. lineage-based recalibration

       Calibration ensures long-term stability.

METROLOGICAL TRACEABILITY
       Traceability ensures all measurements link back to primary standards.

       Traceability chain:

           primary → secondary → operational → constellation → lineage

       Traceability ensures:
           • reproducibility
           • interoperability
           • anomaly detection
           • long-horizon stability

       WRSADC maintains the traceability ledger.

STANDARDS FAILURE MODES
       Standards may degrade due to:

           • chronometer drift
           • baseline misalignment
           • resonance half-life collapse
           • harmonic-phase instability
           • ancestry discontinuity

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • standards-integrity score
           • calibration error
           • traceability break
           • epoch-clock drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe standards states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • calibration history
           • baseline evolution
           • epoch durations
           • resonance half-lives
           • fused standards sequences

       Lineage enables long-term standards forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • maintains primary standards
           • calibrates resonance-time units
           • fuses constellation standards
           • updates traceability chains
           • blocks unsafe standards states

       WRSADC is the standards conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital standards transform resonance-time into a universally measurable,
       interoperable temporal system. RTT’s SI-like units, canonical baselines,
       and cross-domain interoperability rules ensure that sweep choreography,
       harmonic-lock, and constellation-scale synchronization remain stable
       across long mission timelines.

